{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs. realized prices (often by ~5–15 EUR), especially from ~day 5 onward. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: when the recent history is at/near a local high, the model continues projecting elevated/sideways prices, but the market mean-reverts lower. Add a regime check: after a 20–30d upswing, force a declining drift for the next 2–3 weeks.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following sharp run-ups in history (late-30d highs), the model underestimates drawdown magnitude and speed. Increase negative convexity: the higher the last price vs. 30d average, the stronger the downward adjustment (nonlinear).",
    "Horizon-specific error shape: near-term (days 1–3) errors are smaller; mid/long horizon (days 7–30) errors widen materially. Use a horizon-weighted correction: subtract a small amount for days 1–3, larger for days 4–10, largest for days 11–30.",
    "Volatility miscalibration: model paths are too smooth and remain too high; realized paths show sustained selloffs. Increase downside volatility/skew in bearish regimes (e.g., allow step-downs rather than gentle declines).",
    "Mean-reversion anchor: realized outcomes repeatedly revert toward ~70–73 EUR even when forecasts stay in the high-70s/80s. Blend forecasts with a mean-reversion target (e.g., 30d mean or a fixed anchor) with stronger pull as horizon increases."
  ]
}
